WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 125: Martin Van Buren and the Panic of 1837: The Crucible of Martin Van Ruin

1
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:13.920
[Rebecca] Today we conclude our journey through the presidency of Martin Van Buren with the supreme crisis that defined his administration.

4
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.320
[Rebecca] The Panic of 1837.

5
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:23.120
[Rebecca] Just weeks after taking office, Van Buren was hit by the worst financial crash the young nation had ever seen.

6
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.320
[Rebecca] Banks collapsed, unemployment soared, and angry mobs marched in the streets.

7
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:40.000
[Rebecca] Through four years of brutal political attacks, Van Buren refused easy bailouts, fighting tooth and nail to establish a financial system that would protect the public treasury forever.

8
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.040
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full story.

9
00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.880
[George] The day was sunny, crisp, and filled with triumphant celebration.

10
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:05.680
[George] At Van Buren's side rode the departing hero, Andrew Jackson, whose popularity remained colossal.

11
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:11.760
[George] In his inaugural address, Van Buren marveled at the unprecedented prosperity of the young republic.

12
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:21.840
[George] For the first and only time in American history, the national debt had been paid off down to the last penny, leaving a massive financial surplus in the Federal Treasury.

13
00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:29.840
[George] Yet beneath the surface of this golden age, the American economy was intoxicated on wild, unregulated speculation.

14
00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:35.200
[George] When Andrew Jackson destroyed the second bank of the United States in the famous Bank War.

15
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:43.520
[George] Federal deposits were distributed among dozens of politically favored state chartered private banks, derisively dubbed pet banks.

16
00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:53.200
[George] Freed from central bank restraint, these state banks printed vast oceans of unbacked paper bank notes, lending money recklessly to land speculators.

17
00:01:53.200 --> 00:02:03.800
[George] Millions of acres of public lands in Michigan, Illinois, and Mississippi were purchased not by actual settlers, but by speculators using worthless paper promises.

18
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:14.680
[George] Canal companies, turnpike corporations, and state governments borrowed tens of millions of dollars from British investors, building unneeded infrastructure in virgin wilderness.

19
00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:18.840
[George] Cotton prices soared to record highs on speculative euphoria.

20
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:27.080
[George] The entire nation was living in a glittering, precarious house of paper cards, waiting for a single gust of wind to blow it down.

21
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:30.280
[George] The gust of wind arrived from across the Atlantic.

22
00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:41.880
[George] In late 1836, the Bank of England, facing its own catastrophic drain of gold reserves, raised interest rates and sharply curtailed credit to American merchant houses.

23
00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:47.000
[George] British investors demanded immediate repayment of loans in solid gold bullion.

24
00:02:47.000 --> 00:03:04.760
[George] Simultaneously, Andrew Jackson had struck a mortal blow to the paper system with his famous executive order, the Species Circular, mandating that the federal government would accept only gold and silver coin, never paper bank notes, for purchases of public lands on the western frontier.

25
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:06.760
[George] The combination was fatal.

26
00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:16.120
[George] In April 1837, prominent cotton brokerage houses in New Orleans collapsed, dragging down planters and merchant banks across the south.

27
00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:21.000
[George] The panic rushed north along rail and telegraph corridors like an express train.

28
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:36.920
[George] Depositors panicked, forming frantic, weeping mobs outside banks in Philadelphia, Baltimore, Boston, and New York, waving fists full of colorful paper notes and desperately demanding redemption in hard gold eagles and Spanish silver dollars.

29
00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:43.000
[George] On the morning of May 10, 1837, the financial heart of America stopped beating.

30
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:51.600
[George] Every bank in New York City, having completely exhausted its gold vaults, slammed its heavy iron doors and suspended specie payments.

31
00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:58.640
[George] Within days, more than 800 banks across the United States suspended payments or closed permanently.

32
00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:10.960
[George] Paper currency became overnight garbage, merchant ships rotted at their docks, construction halted on hundreds of canals and railroads, and cotton plummeted from twenty cents a pound to eight cents.

33
00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:17.040
[George] Nine out of ten factories in the east closed, throwing thousands into the streets without wages.

34
00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.160
[George] America had plunged headlong into its first great, systemic economic depression.

35
00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.560
[George] The human cost of the panic of 1837 was catastrophic.

36
00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:34.640
[George] In the major cities of the eastern seaboard, nearly one-third of the urban workforce was thrown into immediate, destitute unemployment.

37
00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:41.520
[George] In New York City alone, over 50,000 workers had no income, no savings and no food.

38
00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:49.120
[George] The price of flour and pork skyrocketed as greedy hoarders locked grain in warehouses, waiting for prices to rise further.

39
00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:58.240
[George] In Manhattan, thousands of starving citizens gathered in City Hall Park under black banners reading Bread, meat, rent, and fuel.

40
00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:08.400
[George] Mob leaders pointed toward the great merchant warehouse of Eli Hart and Company on Washington Street, where 53,000 barrels of flour were being hoarded for ransom.

41
00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:21.920
[George] The crowd stormed the building, smashing doors with crowbars, tossing hundreds of barrels of flour and sacks of wheat into the street, and battling armed police and the state militia in what became known as the Great Flower Riots.

42
00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] Similar violent bread riots erupted in Philadelphia, Baltimore, and Boston, while destitute families froze in unheeded tenants.

43
00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:39.000
[George] The Whig opposition party, led by Henry Clay and Daniel Webster, seized upon the economic misery with vicious, partisan glee.

44
00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:43.880
[George] They blamed the entire catastrophe on the policies of Jackson and Van Buren.

45
00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:50.600
[George] Whig newspapers baptized the president with a derisive moniker that stuck like tar, Martin Van Ruin.

46
00:05:50.600 --> 00:06:04.840
[George] Whig cartoonists portrayed Van Buren lounging inside the White House in silk dressing gowns, sipping champagne from French crystal and eating with gold spoons, while honest American mechanics and their children starved in gutters.

47
00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:14.120
[George] Whigs demanded that the president immediately call Congress into session, recharter a national bank, and distribute federal relief to bankrupt businesses.

48
00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:21.480
[George] In September 1837, President Martin Van Buren summoned Congress into a special emergency session.

49
00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:24.360
[George] The political pressure upon him was immense.

50
00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:33.160
[George] Many leaders in his own Democratic Party pleaded with him to yield, print federal paper money, or restore alliances with private state banks.

51
00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:38.760
[George] But Van Buren held fast to a strict, philosophical understanding of the Constitution.

52
00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:45.560
[George] In a courageous, masterly message to Congress, Van Buren laid out his diagnosis of the crisis.

53
00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:49.560
[George] He refused to apologize for Jackson's hard money policies.

54
00:06:49.560 --> 00:07:02.440
[George] The panic, Van Buren argued, was not caused by federal regulations, but by the reckless greed, overbanking, and gambling spirit of private speculators who had built paper empires on debt.

55
00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:09.800
[George] Most controversially, Van Buren asserted that the federal government was not an insurance agency for private misfortune.

56
00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:20.000
[George] In words that became a foundational text of 19th century classical liberalism, Van Buren wrote, All communities are apt to look to government for too much.

57
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:29.040
[George] But it is not the legitimate object of government to aid individuals in the pursuit of private speculation, or to repair losses incurred through improvidence.

58
00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:34.560
[George] The less government interferes with private pursuits, the better for the general prosperity.

59
00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:41.920
[George] Its real duty is to preserve peace, protect property, and leave every citizen free to exert his own industry.

60
00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:45.360
[George] Van Buren refused to bail out the banking cartels.

61
00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:51.200
[George] Instead, he proposed a radical, permanent cure, the complete divorce of bank and state.

62
00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:59.280
[George] Van Buren's revolutionary proposal was the independent treasury, often referred to by contemporaries as the subtreasury system.

63
00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:11.680
[George] For forty years, the federal government had deposited its tax revenues and customs receipts directly into private banks, which used those public funds as collateral to inflate their own speculative loans.

64
00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:19.920
[George] When the banks collapsed in May 1837, millions of dollars of federal money were trapped and frozen inside their locked vaults.

65
00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:26.240
[George] Van Buren declared that the American people's money should never again be used to fuel private speculation.

66
00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:34.560
[George] He proposed that the federal government build its own secure vaults in Washington, New York, Boston, Charleston, and New Orleans.

67
00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:42.720
[George] Federal customs collectors and postmasters would deposit public revenues directly into these federal vaults in gold and silver coin.

68
00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:49.600
[George] The government would pay its debts solely in hard specie, operating entirely independently of private banks.

69
00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:54.400
[George] The banking establishment and the Whig Party reacted with apocalyptic rage.

70
00:08:54.400 --> 00:09:06.600
[George] Henry Clay took to the Senate floor, shouting that the independent treasury was a tyrannical scheme to hoard the nation's gold, starve private business of credit, and reduce the American people to poverty.

71
00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:12.760
[George] Conservative Democrats, closely tied to state banks, bolted the party to oppose the bill.

72
00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:25.880
[George] For three agonizing years, the Independent Treasury Bill was passed by the Senate, defeated by the House, reintroduced, and filibustered in one of the longest, most bitter legislative wars in American history.

73
00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:30.760
[George] Throughout the grueling three-year struggle, Martin Van Buren never flinched.

74
00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:39.000
[George] Demonstrating the tactical patience and quiet resilience that had earned him the title of the Little Magician, he systematically rebuilt his coalition.

75
00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:42.200
[George] He won over his former bitter rival, John C.

76
00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:52.440
[George] Calhoun of South Carolina, by convincing him that an independent Federal Treasury was the ultimate bulwark of states' rights against Northern financial monopolies.

77
00:09:52.440 --> 00:10:05.880
[George] Finally, in June 1840, after three failed attempts, the Independent Treasury Act passed the House of Representatives by a vote of 107 to 107, with the Speaker casting the deciding vote.

78
00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:16.600
[George] On July 4, 1840, the 64th anniversary of the Declaration of Independence, President Martin Van Buren signed the bill into law with solemn pride.

79
00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:21.480
[George] Democrats celebrated the date as America's second declaration of independence.

80
00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:25.800
[George] The declaration of financial independence from private banking empires.

81
00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:29.320
[George] The independent treasury was an institutional milestone.

82
00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:35.640
[George] It established the principle that public funds belonged to the public alone, protected from private greed.

83
00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:40.760
[George] Though the Whigs temporarily repealed the law in 1841, President James K.

84
00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:44.120
[George] Polk reenacted it permanently in 1846.

85
00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:56.320
[George] The Independent Treasury served as the bedrock operating foundation of American federal finance for over 70 years, until it was evolved into the modern Federal Reserve system in 1913.

86
00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:04.400
[George] While the independent treasury was a triumph of principled governance, it came at the ultimate political cost for Martin Van Buren.

87
00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:10.960
[George] The American public was exhausted by years of economic depression, high unemployment, and tight credit.

88
00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:15.680
[George] Voters did not want lectures on fiscal discipline or constitutional limits.

89
00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:19.600
[George] They wanted relief, optimism, and someone to blame.

90
00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:30.480
[George] In the presidential election of 1840, the Whig Party unleashed an unprecedented political carnival against the president, nominating military hero William Henry Harrison.

91
00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:40.640
[George] Van Buren was buried under an avalanche of hard cider barrels, miniature log cabins, and catchy campaign jingles mocking Van, is a used-up man.

92
00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:47.840
[George] Harrison defeated Van Buren in a landslide, winning 234 electoral votes to Van Buren's 60.

93
00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:52.880
[George] Van Buren accepted his defeat with aristocratic grace and philosophical peace.

94
00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:56.720
[George] He refused to compromise his convictions to win an election.

95
00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:10.720
[George] Later in life, Van Buren made another courageous moral stand, breaking with the Democratic Party in 1848 to run for president on the free soil ticket, opposing the expansion of slavery into Western territories.

96
00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:16.960
[George] Martin Van Buren proved that true political leadership is not measured merely by holding on to power.

97
00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:33.720
[George] In the crucible of America's first Great Depression, the Red Fox of Kinderhook chose principle over popularity, safeguarded the Republic's treasury, and proved that a democratic statesman must sometimes endure the wrath of his contemporaries to protect the liberties of future generations.

98
00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:44.200
[Rebecca] Martin Van Buren's steadfast handling of the Panic of 1837 cemented the financial independence of the United States government.

99
00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:50.440
[Rebecca] Though it cost him his presidency, his creation of the independent treasury stood the test of time.

100
00:12:50.440 --> 00:13:07.000
[Rebecca] Next week, we turn to the man who defeated him, America's ninth president, William Henry Harrison, and the legendary log cabin campaign of 1840, a rollicking political carnival that ended with the shortest and most tragic presidency in American history.

101
00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:11.560
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

